ook that might be an amalgam of  multiple people in real life or the  opposite do you make those decisions  based on how it will serve the telling  of the story or how do you evaluate that  well every case is completely different  when you go back to bringing down the  house I think you're probably talking  about the Kevin Spacey character in the  movie 21 or the sort of the professor  yeah character is based on two people so  I had a main character who essentially  brings me the story in most cases and  he's kind of what the book revolves  around so the real guy was named Jeff  Mon bringing down the house and the book  I renamed him Kevin Lewis he was the  center of the story and then everyone  else kind of filled it in as I interview  that and try and get into the story in  terms of how I choose to make a  composite character sometimes it's  because that what I'm asked to do by the  people who are in the book and don't  want people to  know who they are sometimes it's a it's  a mechanism to tell a story that takes  place over a long period of time and  might involve more than one person in  one role so what I like to say is that  all the facts in the story are true but  they're arranged in such a way to bring  out the dramatic flair of the story so  for instance with bringing down the  house I think there were a lot of  articles written about what was true and  what wasn't true yeah and most got it  wrong the things that people thought  weren't true really were true but you  know some of it was written in a very  dramatic way and the dialogue is  recreated because it took place a long  time ago but in terms of that Mickey  Rossa character he was an amalgam of two  people and and it's it suited you know  telling the story of Jeff ma by  composite composing that character so as  far as what was and wasn't true Abbie  I'm curious to hear from you either the  the universally or very commonly pointed  out elements of bringing down the house  and I'm going to just give 