rd now working with George Church  here in the city for the new book  Woolley is there something in particular  I mean I know you live in the city so in  a sense that's what makes a lot of these  people and stories accessible but is  there something unique sort of flavor  that you think is added to these stories  by virtue of them taking place in Boston  yeah I mean I love the city and I also  feel like Boston is kind of the center  of very smart people who sometimes don't  like Authority  I do think there's this whole sort of  feeling among young people and so many  colleges around here and there's so many  people doing biotech and computers and  robots and everything you can kind of  think of that the cutting edge is  happening here so I've always felt this  is a very fertile place I mean I live  here I've lived here for a very long  time and you don't have to go that far  to find a story you know I run into  people every day you have a great story  but in a lot of ways it is also a  coincidence I mean I ran into the MIT  kids and wrote that story then I got an  email out of the blue from Eduardo's  friend who said my best friend founded  Facebook and no one's ever heard of him  and that became the social network and  then this is the the first book I've  written that was really Boston based  since the social network and this one  you know Leigh there's a woolly mammoth  being made right down the street so I  really had to know about it so in a lot  of ways I just run into these stories in  Boston but I feel like if I lived in  another place I wouldn't run into these  sorts of stories so I don't think I'll  ever leave but yeah I do think there's  something very fertile about Boston on  when a bookie right is turned into a  movie is is the thrill of that or I  guess let me phrase it a different way  does does the story possibly being  distorted from the way you wrote it in  some way  bring down what I'm guessing is a pretty  thrilling experience of seeing these  ac